## Architecture

### Session-based async encoding pipeline

1. **POST** `/api/sessions` — Client sends encoding config (renditions, S3 creds, optional webhook URL) with Basic auth
2. Service returns `{ sessionId, uploadUrl, uploadToken }`
3. **POST** `/api/sessions/:id/upload` — Client uploads source file with Bearer token
4. Session enters FIFO queue; webhook sent (if configured) with `queued` status + queue position
5. When session reaches front of queue, FFmpeg encodes to HLS with ABR variants
6. Progress webhooks sent during encoding (~every 5%), or client polls `GET /api/sessions/:id`
7. Output files uploaded to client-specified S3 bucket via MinIO client
8. Final webhook sent with `completed` status + list of S3 object keys + master playlist

### Web client flow

1. User fills in encoding config (type, renditions, S3, optional webhook) in `SessionConfigForm`
2. Selects a file via `FileDropZone` (drag-and-drop or browse)
3. On submit, `App.vue` calls `createSession` then `uploadFile` via `api.ts`
4. `App.vue` computes the S3 public base URL from the S3 config and stores the encoding type
5. Transitions to progress view; `useSessionPoller` polls the API every 2s
6. Polling auto-stops when session reaches a terminal status (`completed` or `failed`)
7. On completion, a Video.js player loads the HLS master playlist from S3 (public URL), with an ABR quality selector and a copy-to-clipboard button for the playlist URL

### FIFO queue

- One encoding job runs at a time
- Sessions processed in first-come-first-served order
- Queue position updates sent via webhook when positions shift

## API Endpoints

| Method | Path | Auth | Description |
|--------|------|------|-------------|
| POST | `/api/sessions` | Basic | Create encoding session |
| POST | `/api/sessions/:sessionId/upload` | Bearer | Upload source media file |
| GET | `/api/sessions/:sessionId` | Basic | Poll session status |

## Session Lifecycle

```
created -> queued -> encoding -> uploading_to_s3 -> completed
                             \-> failed
```

## Key Conventions

- DTOs use `class-validator` decorators and are validated via NestJS `ValidationPipe` (whitelist + forbidNonWhitelisted)
- Webhook configuration is optional in `CreateSessionDto` — when omitted, no webhooks are sent; clients can poll instead
- FFmpeg args are built dynamically based on GPU availability and rendition config
- File uploads use `FileInterceptor` from `@nestjs/platform-express` (10GB limit)
- Environment config via `dotenv`: `PORT`, `AUTH_USERNAME`, `AUTH_PASSWORD`, `WORK_DIR`, `FFMPEG_TIMEOUT_MS`, `CORS_ORIGIN`
- Swagger decorators on all DTOs and endpoints for auto-generated API docs
- Web client stores S3 config in localStorage between sessions for convenience
- Video.js quality selector is a custom plugin (`videojs-quality-selector.ts`) using native ES6 classes — the `videojs-hls-quality-selector` npm package is incompatible with Video.js 8 (Babel `_inheritsLoose` cannot extend native ES6 classes)
- Vite config uses `resolve.dedupe: ['video.js']` to ensure a single Video.js instance across all modules

## GPU Detection

At startup, `FfmpegService.onModuleInit()` checks:
1. `nvidia-smi` is available
2. `ffmpeg -hwaccels` includes `cuda`

When GPU is available: `h264_nvenc` encoder, `scale_cuda` filter, `-hwaccel cuda`
When not: `libx264` encoder, standard `scale` filter

## Scripts

From the repo root:

- `npm run dev` — start both API and web client in dev mode (via `concurrently`)
- `npm -w api run start:dev` — API dev server with watch mode
- `npm -w api run build` — compile API to `api/dist/`
- `npm -w api run start:prod` — run compiled API output
- `npm -w api test` — unit tests
- `npm -w api run test:e2e` — end-to-end tests
- `npm -w app run dev` — web client dev server (Vite, port 5173)
- `npm -w app run build` — production build of the web client
